Cytochrome c is a small, water-soluble hemeprotein (~12 kDa) located in the mitochondrial intermembrane space. It acts as a mobile electron carrier, 
shuttling electrons between Complex III (cytochrome bc1 complex) and Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) during cellular respiration, and triggers programmed
cell death (apoptosis) when released into the cytosol.



Primary Functions

• Cellular Respiration: Accepts an electron from Complex III and diffuses to deliver it to Complex IV, helping build the proton-motive force required 
for ATP synthesis. Learn more via ScienceDirect Topics.
• Apoptosis: Under cellular stress or damage, it leaks into the cytoplasm, binds to Apaf-1 to form the apoptosome, and activates procaspase-9 and 
caspase-3 to dismantle the cell.
• Cardiolipin Interaction: Interacts with inner-mitochondrial cardiolipin, regulating lipid peroxidation and reactive oxygen species (ROS) balance

